  "question": {
    ":question/id": "6a73116a-baf3-516e-87cd-1cccf9b9049f",
    ":question/problem": "Find $AB,$ if $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nand \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $AB=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} | {{field-6}} |  |",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, $A$ is $3\\times 1$ matrix and $B$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix.  \n\nThere is $1$ column in matrix $A$ and $1$ row in matrix $B$, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n{A}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}}{\\cdot }_{✓}{B}_{\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 2}={AB}_{3\\times 2}\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot 2 & 2\\cdot (-3) \\\\ (-3)\\cdot 2 & (-3)\\cdot (-3) \\\\ 4\\cdot 2 & 4\\cdot (-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -12\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
